sun4v-qemu-kit
==============

Firmware for a QEMU-emulated sun4v UltraSPARC T1 machine (-M niagara): the
hypervisor, OpenBoot and the machine descriptions, with the build system and
the tests that make and check them.

QEMU's SPARC64 documentation:
https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/target-sparc64.html

Where to read what
------------------
    README.USAGE         How to use it
    README.configure     ./configure and the build parameters
    REQUIREMENTS.LINUX   TOOLCHAIN=gnu path requirements
    REQUIREMENTS.SOLARIS TOOLCHAIN=sun path requirements
    container/README     build and test through TOOLCHAIN=gnu in a container
    md/README            MD overview & build
    md/REFERENCE         MD: node/property descriptions
    build_matrix         what has been built and run, with which compilers
    tests/README         the test gates and how to run them
    build/hv_reset/README, build/obp/README, build/nvram/README
                         how each part is built
    README.S10U11        the Solaris 10 1/13 DVD image
    LICENSE, LICENSE_CODE, LICENSE_SRC.MAP
                         the licences, and which file is under which


Built from the Sun/Oracle OpenSPARC T2 source release (the distribution
also contains the code for OpenSPARC T1):
https://www.oracle.com/servers/technologies/opensparc-t2-page.html

src/ holds its hypervisor/ and obp/ directories as Sun Microsystems / Oracle
published them, except that the prebuilt binaries in hypervisor-tools/bin are
removed, see PRISTINE-MANIFEST. The hypervisor is built for the T1 platform
ontario (HV_PLATFORM) and OpenBoot for the T1 targets (OBP_PLATFORM).

The build never modifies src/: it applies patches/ to a copy.

Only the single-strand configuration (1up-*.bin) is built and supported,
because QEMU's niagara machine has one CPU.


The firmware set
----------------
qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara loads six files, named in hw/sparc64/niagara.c,
from the -L directory:

    reset.bin      CPU(s) reset vector(s); starts the hypervisor (hv)
    q.bin          the hypervisor (hv)
    openboot.bin   OpenBoot PROM (OBP)
    1up-hv.bin     hv machine description (MD): the platform part
    1up-md.bin     guest MD: the domain, OBP/OS visible
    nvram1         NVRAM image: an empty stub; required by QEMU

How to run them under QEMU: README.USAGE.


Toolchains
----------
Every component builds two ways. TOOLCHAIN picks one (`sun` or `gnu`); the
default follows `uname -s`.

    sun   Solaris/SPARC with Sun's own makefiles and the system assembler.
          Sun Studio 11 was used for the first builds of this tree.
          See REQUIREMENTS.SOLARIS.
    gnu   GNU/Linux with the sparc64-linux-gnu cross toolchain. No Sun
          software. See REQUIREMENTS.LINUX.

Without the sparc64 cross toolchain and qemu-user, build in a container:
container/README.

The gnu and sun paths give byte-identical 1up-hv.bin and 1up-md.bin. The other
files depend on the toolchain and the build: do not compare their hashes
between builds. q.bin also carries the git revision in its banner, so it
changes with every commit. The release tarball differs between distributions
(it carries q.bin and openboot.bin) and is identical within one.
Details: build_matrix, build/hv_reset/README, build/obp/README.

./configure is optional: it probes the host's compilers and tools for the sun or
gnu path, and sets the build's parameters. README.configure.


Machine descriptions: mdgen, mdlint
-----------------------------------
A machine description (MD) is the binary graph a sun4v hypervisor hands to
its guest to describe the hardware: CPUs, memory, I/O, interrupts.
The hv reads its own MD (1up-hv.bin) for the platform. OBP builds its device
tree from the guest MD (1up-md.bin), and the kernel of Solaris (and its
derivatives) builds its kernel structures from it during init. The MD is made
of typed nodes with named properties, joined by forward and back arcs.

Format: FWARC 2005/115
https://sun4v.github.io/ARChive/FWARC/2005/115/final.materials/index.html

An introduction:
https://unix0.cc/2026/07/16/sun4v-machine-description-part-2/

Sun's `mdgen` compiles the MD text source into that binary. `mdlint` checks a
binary and, with `-t`, turns it back into text. Building the MD pair (next
section) needs `mdgen`; `make md` builds both tools first. You can build them
alone:

    make mdtools                     # $WORKDIR/mdtools/{mdgen,mdlint}


Building the MD pair (*-md.bin/*-hv.bin)
----------------------------------------
md/ holds one machine per directory, md/<target>/; TARGET=<target> picks
it. What each machine is, what boots on it and the matching QEMU command
line: md/README and md/<target>/README.

Where <target> is:
    legion   Sun's Legion simulator devices: an hsimd disk, a console and
             an RTC. Runs on upstream QEMU. The default.
    pci      a PCIe root nexus with an e1000 NIC and a cmd646 IDE controller.
             Relies on patches/ (applied at build time) and a QEMU with
             vpci and cmd646 support (not in upstream yet).

    make md                          # legion (the default)
    make md TARGET=<target>       # that machine
    make md TARGET=all            # every machine, each in $OUTDIR/<target>/

Output: $OUTDIR/{1up-md.bin,1up-hv.bin}.

The guest RAM is part of the MD (for legion 2048 MB). To change it:
./configure --with-md-mem=MB or make MD_MEM=MB. QEMU's -m must be the same
number (README.USAGE). See README.configure.


Hypervisor and reset vector: q.bin, reset.bin
---------------------------------------------
The hypervisor (q.bin) and its reset vectors (reset.bin) are written mostly in
SPARC assembler, using the hyperprivileged registers and instructions of
SPARC V9d. How they are built: build/hv_reset/README.

    make hv_reset                    # $OUTDIR/{q.bin,reset.bin}

Compile options: build/hv_reset/README.options.


OpenBoot PROM: openboot.bin
---------------------------
openboot.bin is built by a Forth bootstrap (see diagram docs/obp-chain.png).
It starts from one original binary of Sun's archive,
./src/obp/obp/os/sun/sparc/forth.dic; every other dictionary is built from
source. Both toolchain paths run the same chain, but the gnu path runs it under
qemu-sparc32plus and qemu-sparc64 (linux-user): the dictionaries are SPARC
machine code (REQUIREMENTS.LINUX).
Details and proof: build/obp/README.
An experimental way to start without forth.dic: forth_bootstrap/README.

    make obp                         # $OUTDIR/openboot.bin
    make obp-verify                  # `gnu` only: compare with Sun's own
                                     # images (tests/full/obp/TESTS)

TARGET also selects the OBP build: niagara/t1_fpga for legion,
niagara1-hw/release for pci. See build/obp/README.


NVRAM image: nvram1
-------------------
    make nvram                       # $OUTDIR/nvram1

nvram1 is NVRAM_SIZE zero bytes. This is the correct content: the hypervisor,
OBP and the MD never read it. QEMU still requires the file (niagara.c loads it
with add_rom_or_fail()). OBP's configuration -- boot-device, console,
devaliases -- comes from the guest MD. build/nvram/README.

